xnu 0344: the window server can carry the injector, and the CP stress is opt-in

CoreAnimation composes in `backboardd` and picks its renderer once per process:
Metal, else OpenGL ES, else software. This port publishes no Metal device, so
the GLES renderer is the only accelerated one, and the only library that turns
it over to Mesa is the injector; the hook in the same process is what takes the
window server off the CPU. Measured 2026-11-17 with `mondrian_ui_hook=4`: the
compositor runs `mode copy`, every `MONDRIAN MEM` sample reads `gpu 0MiB/0`, and
no process in the display path loads the injector.

`mondrian_gpu_hook=1` gives the bit 2 target
`/private/var/vm/mondrian-inject.dylib` -- the copy `glblob=1` writes -- instead
of the hook. When that blob is not there `backboardd` gets nothing, rather than
falling back to the hook. Bit 2 without the argument is unchanged.

The CP bring-up stress (2 000 submissions) is now off unless `cpstress=1`: it is
a diagnostic, and on a boot whose ring has anything wrong one submission costs
its 2 s timeout, while on a healthy boot it only fills the ring the submission
path is read from.

